# Caddyfile.example — reverse proxy for the gpx.studio embed POC.
#
# Routes the TWO app domains to their Vite dev servers. The bridge backend
# is proxied *inside* Vite (the shell's /api → :3001), so this proxy is just
# two dumb routes — no /api or service hops to configure here.
#
# Setup: replace <server-ip> with the machine running the dev servers
# (e.g. `hostname -I`) and example.com with your domain.

# --- Editor: whole domain to itself, so its root-relative assets
#     (/_app, /@vite, …) resolve cleanly with no path mangling ---
http://gpxstudio.example.com {
	reverse_proxy <server-ip>:5180
}

# --- Bridge shell: serves the host UI and proxies /api → backend
#     internally via Vite ---
http://gpx.example.com {
	reverse_proxy <server-ip>:5174
}

# --- TLS ---
# The http:// prefixes above make Caddy serve plain HTTP. For HTTPS, drop
# the http:// scheme from the site addresses and Caddy auto-provisions
# Let's Encrypt certs (needs ports 80/443 reachable).
#
# For a wildcard cert, or when port 80 is closed, use the DNS challenge.
# Example with the Cloudflare plugin (must be compiled into your Caddy
# binary — check with `caddy list-modules | grep cloudflare`):
#
#   (tls) {
#   	tls {
#   		dns cloudflare {env.CF_API_TOKEN}
#   		resolvers 1.1.1.1
#   	}
#   }
#   # then `import tls` inside each site block above
#
# When switching to HTTPS, also flip the origin-bearing env vars
# (VITE_EDITOR_URL, VITE_EMBED_ALLOWED_ORIGINS) to https://. The relative
# VITE_*_URL service paths are scheme-agnostic and need no change.
